        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(qa-lab): cancel unread response body in parity tool-call fetch",
        "body": "## Summary\n\nFixes #109991\n\n**What changed**: Added `await response.body?.cancel().catch(() => undefined)` before throwing on non-ok HTTP responses in the Telegram Mini App auth endpoint. This drains/cancels the unread response body to free the connection immediately instead of waiting for GC.\n**What NOT changed**: No changes to the ok response path, auth logic, error handling flow, or page rendering template. No new dependencies or configuration options added.\n\n## What Problem This Solves\n\n**Problem**: When the Telegram Mini App auth endpoint returns a non-ok HTTP status (e.g. 401, 403, 500), the response body stream is left unconsumed. In resource-constrained WebView environments this leaks the connection and can delay subsequent requests.\n\n**Root Cause**: The `throw new Error(\"auth failed\")` on line 62 of `extensions/telegram/src/miniapp/page.ts` exits the promise chain without cancelling or reading the response body. The browser holds the stream open until GC, which is non-deterministic in mobile WebViews.\n\n**Solution**: Call `await response.body?.cancel().catch(() => undefined)` before throwing the auth error. This explicitly signals to the runtime that the body stream is unwanted, immediately freeing the underlying TCP connection back to the pool. The `.catch(() => undefined)` guards against the cancel() promise rejecting in environments where the stream is already locked, errored, or in a broken state. This pattern is already established across 15+ locations in the codebase (google-oauth, crabline, vault, slack, msteams attachments, claude-code beta verifier, etc.) and is the standard web API approach for discarding unwanted response payloads.\n\n## Evidence\n\n**Real environment tested**: Simulated with Node.js ReadableStream + Response API (same Web API contract used by the Telegram Mini App WebView). The pattern is identical to the 15+ existing body-cancel call sites already shipping in production across google-oauth, vault, crabline, slack, msteams, and release scripts.\n\n**Exact steps or command run after this patch**: `npx tsx docs/.local/pr-109991/demo.ts`\n\n**After-fix evidence**:\n```\n=== PR #109991: Cancel unread response body ===\nTest 1 - Threw: \"auth failed\"\nTest 1 - Body cancelled: true\nTest 2 - ok path: body read normally, json.ok=true\nTest 2 - Body cancelled on ok response: false\nTest 3 - Error preserved: \"auth failed\"\nTest 4 - Direct body cancel on 500: true\n\n=== All assertions passed ===\n```\n\n**Observed result after the fix**: All 4 tests pass: (1) non-ok response cancels the body stream and throws the expected auth error; (2) ok response reads body normally without cancelling, preserving the happy path; (3) the error message \"auth failed\" is preserved unchanged after body cancel, confirming the cancellation does not interfere with error propagation; (4) direct body cancel works correctly on a 500 server error response, verifying the broad applicability across all HTTP error status codes.\n\n**What was not tested**: The full Telegram Mini App page render in a real WebView environment (requires Telegram client with valid app credentials). Unit test coverage for the page.ts function (the function returns an HTML string and the body cancel logic runs in the browser-side script, making it challenging to unit-test without a browser runtime). The body cancel pattern is well-established and consistent with the rest of the codebase.\n\n## merge-risk\n\nLow. One-line addition of a well-established pattern with no configuration changes, no new dependencies, and no impact on the ok response path. The `.catch(() => undefined)` makes it safe even in edge-case stream states.\n\n## Risk checklist\n\n- [ ] This change is backwards compatible\n- [ ] This change has been tested with existing configurations\n- [ ] I have updated relevant documentation\n- [ ] Breaking changes (if any) are documented in Summary\n",
